🔰 1. What is Spring Cloud Gateway?
Spring Cloud Gateway is an API Gateway built on Spring WebFlux (reactive).
It is the single entry point for microservices and handles routing plus cross-cutting concerns
(security, logging, rate limiting, path rewrite, load balancing).

Simple analogy: traffic cop / receptionist for backend services.

⚠️ Reminder: Gateway is WebFlux-based → do not put `spring-boot-starter-web` on the same app.


🔰 2. Why use Spring Cloud Gateway in Microservices?
Clients should not call every service directly.
Gateway gives:
- Single entry point
- Central place for auth, logging, rate limit, path rewrite
- Routing to the correct service
- Optional discovery + load balancing (`lb://`)


🔰 3. What is a Route?
A Route = rule for handling a request. It has:
- id
- predicates (match conditions)
- uri (where to forward: `http://...` or `lb://service-name`)
- filters (optional request/response changes)

```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: user-service
          uri: lb://user-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/users/**
          filters:
            - StripPrefix=1
```


🔰 4. What are Predicates?
Predicates = conditions that decide whether a route matches.
If predicates match → that route is used.

Examples:
- `Path=/api/**`
- `Method=GET`
- `Header=X-Request-Id, \d+`
- `Host=*.mydomain.com`

Multiple predicates on one route are combined with AND.


🔰 5. What are Filters?
Filters modify the request/response or add behavior:
- add headers
- strip/rewrite path
- auth checks
- rate limiting
- logging

Interview wording:
- “before forwarding” work happens before `chain.filter(...)`
- “after response” work happens in `.then(...)` / post logic

Common built-in filters: `AddRequestHeader`, `StripPrefix`, `RewritePath`, `RequestRateLimiter`, `CircuitBreaker`


🔰 6. Global vs Route-specific filters?
| Type            | Applies to              | How configured                         |
| --------------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Route-specific  | Only one route          | Under that route’s `filters:` in YAML  |
| Global          | Almost every request    | `GlobalFilter` bean in Java            |

```yaml
filters:
  - AddRequestHeader=X-Request-Foo, Bar
```


🔰 7. How to define routes?
Two ways:

a) YAML
```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: product-service
          uri: lb://product-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/products/**
```

b) Java `RouteLocator`
```java
@Bean
public RouteLocator customRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
    return builder.routes()
        .route("product-service", r -> r.path("/products/**")
            .uri("lb://product-service"))
        .build();
}
```


🔰 8. Spring MVC vs Spring WebFlux?
| Feature       | Spring MVC                 | Spring WebFlux                    |
| ------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Thread model  | Blocking (Servlet)         | Non-blocking / reactive           |
| Server        | Tomcat/Jetty (typical)     | Netty (typical)                   |
| Best for      | Traditional request/thread | High concurrency, streaming       |

Gateway uses WebFlux → non-blocking gateway processing.


🔰 9. How to enable service discovery with Gateway?
1) Add Eureka client (+ LoadBalancer)
2) Register Gateway as Eureka client
3) Use discovery-aware URI:

```yaml
uri: lb://user-service
```

Meaning:
Resolve `user-service` from Eureka, then choose one instance via Spring Cloud LoadBalancer.

Optional shortcut:
```yaml
spring.cloud.gateway.discovery.locator.enabled: true
```
auto-exposes `/user-service/**` routes.
Not mandatory if you already define explicit `routes:`.


🔰 10. What is Rate Limiting?
Limits how many requests a client can make in a time window.

Gateway common approach: `RequestRateLimiter` + Redis.

```yaml
filters:
  - name: RequestRateLimiter
    args:
      redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10   # steady rate
      redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20   # burst limit
```
One line:
replenishRate: 10 = allow 10 requests per second steadily;
burstCapacity: 20 = temporarily allow up to 20 if tokens were unused (short burst).

Also need:
- Redis
- a `KeyResolver` bean (e.g. by IP / user id)


🔰 11. How to create a custom filter?
Implement `GlobalFilter` (all routes) or route `GatewayFilter` / filter factory.

```java
@Component
public class LoggingFilter implements GlobalFilter {
    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        System.out.println("Request URI: " + exchange.getRequest().getURI());
        return chain.filter(exchange);
    }
}
```


🔰 12. How to implement Circuit Breaker in Gateway?
Use Resilience4j gateway filter (needs circuitbreaker dependency):

```yaml
filters:
  - name: CircuitBreaker
    args:
      name: myCircuitBreaker
      fallbackUri: forward:/fallback
```

If downstream fails / circuit opens → request goes to fallback endpoint.


🔰 13. How does Gateway support load balancing?
With:
```text
uri: lb://user-service
```
+ Eureka (or other discovery) + Spring Cloud LoadBalancer.

Gateway gets instance list, LoadBalancer picks one.

⚠️ Ribbon is legacy/removed in modern Spring Cloud. Say LoadBalancer in interviews.


🔰 14. How to implement JWT authentication in Gateway?
Common pattern:
- Custom GlobalFilter / AuthenticationWebFilter validates JWT
- Reject invalid tokens at gateway
- Optionally forward user claims via headers to downstream services

Or integrate Spring Security OAuth2 Resource Server on the gateway.

What does “forward user claims via headers” mean?
Claims = data inside JWT (e.g. userId, email, role).

After Gateway validates the JWT, it can put that info into HTTP headers before calling the microservice:

```text
Client → Gateway (validates JWT)
            ↓ adds headers, e.g.
              X-User-Id: 42
              X-User-Role: ADMIN
            ↓
         Order Service reads headers (doesn’t need to parse JWT again)
```

Why:
Gateway does auth once; downstream services just read headers for “who is this user?”

Optional:
You can also make every service validate JWT itself; header-forwarding is a common shortcut.


🔰 15. How to enable CORS in Spring Cloud Gateway?

What is CORS?
CORS = Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

Browsers block frontend JS on one origin from calling an API on another origin by default.
Example:
- Frontend: `http://localhost:3000`
- Gateway/API: `http://localhost:8080`
Different origin (host/port) → browser requires CORS permission.

Server (Gateway) must allow it by sending CORS headers, e.g.:
- which origins can call (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`)
- which methods/headers are allowed
- whether cookies/auth credentials are allowed

Interview one-liner:
CORS is a browser security rule; Gateway CORS config tells the browser “this frontend is allowed to call my API.”

Use `CorsWebFilter` (or gateway `globalcors` config).

⚠️ Important: `allowCredentials(true)` cannot be combined with `allowedOrigin("*")`.
Use specific origins or `allowedOriginPatterns`.

```java
@Bean
public CorsWebFilter corsWebFilter() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();
    config.setAllowCredentials(true);
    config.addAllowedOriginPattern("http://localhost:3000"); // not "*"
    config.addAllowedHeader("*");
    config.addAllowedMethod("*");

    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    source.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", config);
    return new CorsWebFilter(source);
}
```

YAML alternative:
```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      globalcors:
        cors-configurations:
          '[/**]':
            allowedOriginPatterns: "http://localhost:3000"
            allowedMethods: "*"
            allowedHeaders: "*"
            allowCredentials: true
```


🔰 16. Scenario: log request and response?
Use a GlobalFilter:

- “before forwarding” work happens before `chain.filter(...)`
- “after response” work happens in `.then(...)` / post logic

```java
@Component
public class LoggingFilter implements GlobalFilter {
    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        System.out.println("Request: " + exchange.getRequest().getURI());
        return chain.filter(exchange)
            .then(Mono.fromRunnable(() ->
                System.out.println("Response: " + exchange.getResponse().getStatusCode())
            ));
    }
}
```


🔰 17. Predicate vs Filter? (very common)
| Concept    | Question it answers              |
| ---------- | -------------------------------- |
| Predicate  | Should this route match?         |
| Filter     | What extra logic should run?     |


🔰 18. Gateway vs Eureka?
| Component | Role                                      |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Eureka    | Service registry (phone-book)             |
| Gateway   | Front door / router (can use the phone-book via `lb://`) |


🔰 19. Gateway vs Zuul?
| Point        | Zuul (older Netflix)     | Spring Cloud Gateway        |
| ------------ | ------------------------ | --------------------------- |
| Stack        | Servlet (Zuul 1)         | WebFlux reactive            |
| Status       | Legacy in Spring Cloud   | Modern recommended gateway  |


🔰 20. What does StripPrefix do?
Removes path parts before forwarding.

Example:
Incoming: `/users/list`  
`StripPrefix=1` → forwards `/list` to downstream service.
